  "constraintReading": "len(height) can reach 10⁵, so trying every pair means about 5·10⁹ area checks: O(n²) is far too slow. Aim for O(n), one inward pass that rules out a wall at each step. Only two indexes and the best area are stored, so the space is O(1).",
  "hints": {
    "clarify": "Do the walls in between lower the water? No: only the two chosen walls matter, and the shorter one sets the level. Try [2, 7, 1, 3] by hand: walls 0 and 3 hold min(2, 3) × 3 = 6, walls 1 and 3 hold min(7, 3) × 2 = 6, and walls 0 and 1 hold only min(2, 7) × 1 = 2. The best is 6.",
    "approach": "Two pointers from opposite ends. Start with the widest tank, the two outer walls, and measure it. Then move one pointer inward. Which one? The shorter wall caps the water, and every tank it could make with a wall further in is narrower and still capped by it, so it can never beat the tank you just measured. Drop it: move its pointer inward. Keep the best area as you go, until the pointers meet.",
    "whyNot": "Measuring every pair of walls is O(n²): about 5·10⁹ pairs for 10⁵ walls. Sorting the walls by height loses their positions, and the distance between them is half of the area. Moving the pointer at the taller wall can discard the best tank, since the shorter wall still caps everything you measure next.",
    "slots": {
      "setup": "`l` on the first wall, `r` on the last, and the best area so far at 0.",
      "loop": "Keep going while `l` is left of `r`; when they meet, no tank is left to measure.",
      "update": "Move the pointer at the shorter wall inward. When the two walls are equal, moving either is fine: neither can be part of a bigger tank with a wall further in.",
      "record": "Measure the tank between `l` and `r`: the shorter of the two heights times `r − l`, and keep it if it beats the best.",
      "return": "Return the best area once the pointers meet."
    }
  },
  "solution": {
    "code": "class Solution:\n    def maxArea(self, height: list[int]) -> int:\n        l, r = 0, len(height) - 1\n        best = 0\n        while l < r:\n            water = min(height[l], height[r]) * (r - l)\n            best = max(best, water)  # viz:measure\n            if height[l] < height[r]:\n                l += 1  # viz:move_l\n            else:\n                r -= 1  # viz:move_r\n        return best  # viz:done\n",
    "explanation": "Each step moves one pointer one wall inward, so after at most n − 1 steps they meet: O(n) time. Only `l`, `r`, `water` and `best` are stored: O(1) space. Dropping the shorter wall is safe because every tank it could still form is narrower than the one just measured, and no taller than that wall, so none of them can hold more.",
    "toolkit": []
  },
